<nav class="pnav"><a class="plogo" href="01 - the-project-individual.html">⬡ PRIME</a><span class="pdigits">1·3·7·9</span><a class="nl" href="00 - PRIME INDEX.html">INDEX ⌕</a></nav>
<h1 id="global-human-knowledge-and-experience-archive">⬡ GLOBAL HUMAN KNOWLEDGE AND EXPERIENCE ARCHIVE</h1>
<h2 id="design-specification-knowledge_archivemd">Design Specification — knowledge_archive.md</h2>
<p><em>Version 1.0 · Synthesizes the parallel classification maps (<code>math_logic_map.md</code>, <code>ml_pipeline_guide.md</code>, et al.) into one unified architecture.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Mission statement.</strong> This is not a Wikipedia clone and not a vector store.
It is the <strong>institutional memory of human civilization</strong>: a permanently maintained,
verifiably sourced, cross-disciplinary record of <em>everything humanity has
discovered, proved, designed, built, tried, and failed</em> — designed so that the
next person (or next civilization) does not repeat the mistakes of the last.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Map family:</strong>
<a class="doc" href="40 - map-engineering-medicine.html">🧬 map-engineering-medicine</a>
<a class="doc" href="41 - map-cs-it.html">💻 map-cs-it</a>
<a class="doc" href="42 - map-math-logic.html">∑ map-math-logic</a>
<a class="doc" href="43 - map-natural-sciences.html">🔬 map-natural-sciences</a>
<a class="doc" href="44 - map-ai-ml.html">🤖 map-ai-ml</a>
<a class="doc" href="45 - map-cybersecurity.html">🛡️ map-cybersecurity</a>
<a class="doc" href="47 - map-humanities-society.html">🧑‍🤝‍🧑 map-humanities-society</a>
<a class="doc" href="00 - PRIME INDEX.html">⬡ PRIME INDEX — all maps</a>
</p>
<hr />
<h2 id="0-design-philosophy-why-this-must-exist">0. DESIGN PHILOSOPHY — why this must exist</h2>
<h3 id="01-the-problem-today">0.1 The problem today</h3>
<p>Human knowledge is currently scattered, decaying, locked, and unreliable:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Failure mode</th>
<th>Real example</th>
<th>Cost</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Knowledge trapped in heads / proprietary systems</td>
<td>Proprietary trade secrets, unpublished lab notebooks</td>
<td>Re-derivation of known results; duplicated research</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Knowledge lost to media decay</td>
<td>War losses, burned libraries (Alexandria, House of Wisdom), endangered oral traditions</td>
<td>Entire fields have to be rediscovered</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Knowledge frozen in languages few read</td>
<td>Science in inaccessible journals, patents in dead formats</td>
<td>Global south excluded from frontier knowledge</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Knowledge fragmented across silos</td>
<td>Physics on arXiv, medicine on PubMed, history in archives — no cross-links</td>
<td>Disciplinary blind spots; slow cross-pollination</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Failed knowledge deleted</td>
<td>Negative results unpublished, failed experiments forgotten</td>
<td><strong>Science itself repeats mistakes</strong> — the single biggest waste</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Knowledge with no provenance</td>
<td>Wikipedia citations rot, AI hallucinations fabricate sources</td>
<td>Trust collapse</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3 id="02-what-experience-means-here">0.2 What "experience" means here</h3>
<p>An archive of <em>discoveries</em> without <em>experiences</em> is a museum of answers.
The archive must preserve the <strong>process</strong>: the context, the assumptions that
turned out wrong, the resources that failed, the errors that cost lives,
the partial successes, the lessons distilled. The <strong>failed experiment is
first-class knowledge</strong> — arguably the most valuable knowledge, because it
is the knowledge that prevents the next failure.</p>
<p>A surgeon who knows <em>why</em> the Thalidomide disaster happened will not repeat
it. A chemist who knows <em>which</em> esterifications polymerized catastrophically
will avoid them. The archive is a <strong>negative-knowledge engine</strong> as much as
a positive-knowledge engine.</p>
<h3 id="03-design-principles-the-prime-ladder-foundations-first">0.3 Design principles (the PRIME ladder — foundations first)</h3>
<pre><code>1 · 3 · 7 · 9
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li><strong>1 · Provenance first.</strong> Every claim carries its source chain. No orphan
  knowledge enters the canonical layer.</li>
<li><strong>3 · Three pillars.</strong> Knowledge content + Knowledge graph + Experience
  records. All three required; the graph alone is a diagram, the text alone
  is a pile, the experience alone is a diary.</li>
<li><strong>7 · Seven verification tiers</strong> (Section 3). Every node labeled.</li>
<li><strong>9 · Nine imperatives:</strong> completeness · verifiability · multilingual ·
  offline-capable · open · uncapturable · auditable · evolving · free.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2 id="1-core-mission">1. CORE MISSION</h2>
<h3 id="11-preserve-the-seven-knowledge-classes">1.1 Preserve (the seven knowledge classes)</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>#</th>
<th>Class</th>
<th>Examples</th>
<th>Why preserved</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>1</td>
<td><strong>Discoveries</strong></td>
<td>laws of physics, new species, historical finds</td>
<td>The frontier of the known</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td><strong>Mathematics</strong></td>
<td>proofs, theorems, algorithms, formal systems</td>
<td>The language of reasoning itself</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td><strong>Experiences</strong></td>
<td>lived histories, oral traditions, field notes</td>
<td>Context that papers strip out</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>4</td>
<td><strong>History</strong></td>
<td>events, causality chains, failed states</td>
<td>Pattern memory of societies</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>5</td>
<td><strong>Inventions</strong></td>
<td>patents, designs, prototypes, trade secrets (with consent)</td>
<td>Applied capability</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>6</td>
<td><strong>Experiments</strong></td>
<td>methods, results, <em>and failures</em>, negative results</td>
<td>The scientific method's full record</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>7</td>
<td><strong>Solutions</strong></td>
<td>engineering fixes, medical protocols, legal precedents</td>
<td>Reusable resolution patterns</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Plus, continuously: <strong>culture</strong> (art, religion, language, myth), <strong>lessons</strong>
(distilled warnings), <strong>designs</strong> (architecture, UI, systems).</p>
<h3 id="12-the-anti-repeat-mistake-guarantee">1.2 The Anti-Repeat-Mistake guarantee</h3>
<p>The archive must answer, for any domain, the question: <em>"What has been tried
before, and how did it fail?"</em> This requires:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Failure records are mandatory</strong> for experiment-type nodes (Section 5).</li>
<li><strong>Failure nodes are cross-linked</strong> to the methods they invalidate — a
   graph edge <code>INVALIDATES(method → prior_result)</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Lessons are first-class nodes</strong> linked to every failure they distill.</li>
<li><strong>Retrieval must surface failures</strong> alongside successes — a search for
   "fusion reactor design" returns the failed tokamaks with their failure
   modes <em>before</em> the proposed design (Section 4.6).</li>
</ol>
<h3 id="13-non-goals">1.3 Non-goals</h3>
<ul>
<li>Not a replacement for the live literature — an <em>index and memory layer</em> over it.</li>
<li>Not an opinion platform — claims require evidence (Section 3).</li>
<li>Not a single centralized corporate product — governance is federated (Section 7).</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2 id="2-knowledge-hierarchy-classification-framework">2. KNOWLEDGE HIERARCHY &amp; CLASSIFICATION FRAMEWORK</h2>
<h3 id="21-the-canonical-spine">2.1 The canonical spine</h3>
<pre><code>DOMAIN → DISCIPLINE → SUBDISCIPLINE → FIELD → CONCEPT → PRINCIPLE →
METHOD → EXPERIMENT → EVIDENCE → APPLICATION → OUTCOME
</code></pre>
<p>This is a <strong>containment + derivation</strong> hierarchy, not just a taxonomy.
Each level has a defined semantics:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Level</th>
<th>Definition</th>
<th>Example (energy domain)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Domain</strong></td>
<td>Top-level human-knowledge area</td>
<td><code>Domain: Natural Sciences</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Discipline</strong></td>
<td>Recognized academic/professional field</td>
<td><code>Discipline: Physics</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Subdiscipline</strong></td>
<td>Major branch</td>
<td><code>Subdiscipline: Plasma Physics</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Field</strong></td>
<td>Specific research/practice area</td>
<td><code>Field: Magnetic Confinement Fusion</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Concept</strong></td>
<td>Atomic idea/entity</td>
<td><code>Concept: Tokamak</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Principle</strong></td>
<td>Generalized law/pattern</td>
<td><code>Principle: Lawson Criterion</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Method</strong></td>
<td>Reproducible procedure</td>
<td><code>Method: Neutral Beam Injection</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Experiment</strong></td>
<td>A specific attempt with context</td>
<td><code>Experiment: JET D-T campaign 1997</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Evidence</strong></td>
<td>Observation/datum supporting a claim</td>
<td><code>Evidence: JET achieved 16 MW fusion power</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Application</strong></td>
<td>Deployed use</td>
<td><code>Application: ITER design basis</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Outcome</strong></td>
<td>Measured result / status</td>
<td><code>Outcome: Q=0.67 achieved; gain not yet &gt;1</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3 id="22-cross-cutting-axes-orthogonal-to-the-spine">2.2 Cross-cutting axes (orthogonal to the spine)</h3>
<p>The spine is one axis. Every node also carries:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Epistemic tier</strong> (Section 3) — how confident we are.</li>
<li><strong>Knowledge class</strong> (Section 1.1) — what <em>kind</em> of knowledge it is.</li>
<li><strong>Time axis</strong> — <code>discovered_at</code>, <code>superseded_by</code>, <code>valid_from/until</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Space/culture axis</strong> — region, tradition, language of origin.</li>
<li><strong>Confidence &amp; controversy flags</strong> — disputed, consensus, fringe.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="23-the-classification-maps-unify-here">2.3 The classification maps unify here</h3>
<p>The parallel maps (<code>math_logic_map.md</code> covers math/logic; <code>ml_pipeline_guide.md</code>
covers ML pipelines) plug into this spine at specific levels:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>math_logic_map</strong> → <code>Domain: Formal Sciences → Discipline: Mathematics /
  Logic / Computer Science → ... → Concept: e.g. Gödel Incompleteness →
  Principle: ... → Method: proof technique → Experiment: formal verification</code>.</li>
<li><strong>ml_pipeline_guide</strong> → <code>Domain: Applied Sciences → Discipline:
  Computer Science → Subdiscipline: Machine Learning → Field: e.g. RAG
  pipelines → Concept: embedding → Principle: ... → Method: fine-tuning →
  Experiment: eval run → Evidence: benchmark scores → Application: deployed
  model → Outcome: measured quality</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>New maps (biology, history, medicine, law, arts) slot in identically.
<strong>The spine is domain-agnostic by design.</strong></p>
<h3 id="24-knowledge-graph-schema">2.4 Knowledge graph schema</h3>
<p><strong>Node types</strong> (prefix <code>n:</code>):</p>
<pre><code>n:Domain        n:Discipline     n:Subdiscipline  n:Field
n:Concept       n:Principle      n:Method         n:Experiment
n:Evidence      n:Application    n:Outcome        n:Person
n:Organization  n:Place          n:Event          n:Artifact
n:Document      n:Dataset        n:Formula        n:Theorem
n:Algorithm     n:Protocol       n:Failure        n:Lesson
n:Language      n:Tradition      n:Myth           n:Question  (open problems)
n:Controversy   n:Hypothesis     n:Prediction     n:Tool
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Edge types</strong> (prefix <code>e:</code>), grouped:</p>
<p><em>Containment / derivation:</em></p>
<pre><code>PART_OF          (child → parent, up the spine)
PART_OF_FIELD    (cross-cutting membership)
DERIVED_FROM     (downstream node ← upstream basis)
SUPERSEDES       (newer node replaces older)
INSTANCE_OF      (specific → general type)
</code></pre>
<p><em>Causal / evidential:</em></p>
<pre><code>EVIDENCES        (evidence → claim it supports)
CONTRADICTS      (evidence vs. claim, or node vs. node)
SUPPORTS         (weak/partial support)
INVALIDATES      (failure → method/principle it rules out)
CAUSED_BY        (outcome ← cause)
ENABLES          (capability dependency)
DEPENDS_ON       (prerequisite)
</code></pre>
<p><em>Temporal / provenance:</em></p>
<pre><code>PUBLISHED_IN     (document → venue/journal)
AUTHORED_BY      (node → person/org)
OBSERVED_AT      (evidence → place/time)
CONDUCTED_BY     (experiment → team/org)
USES             (experiment → method/tool/dataset)
CITES            (document → document)
TRANSLATED_FROM  (node → source-language version)
</code></pre>
<p><em>Social / cultural:</em></p>
<pre><code>INFLUENCED_BY    (person/node ← person/node)
TEACHES          (person → concept/lesson)
PRACTICED_IN     (tradition → region)
RELATED_TO       (weak semantic link, high fan-out — used sparingly)
</code></pre>
<p>Every edge carries: <code>weight</code> (confidence 0..1), <code>source</code> (which evidence
chain justifies the edge), <code>valid_from</code>, <code>valid_until</code>, <code>created_by</code>,
<code>verification_tier</code>.</p>
<h3 id="25-open-questions-as-nodes">2.5 Open questions as nodes</h3>
<p>Unsolved problems are <code>n:Question</code> nodes — <em>"What is dark matter?"</em>,
<em>"P vs NP?"</em>, <em>"How did the Indus script work?"</em>. They link <code>OPEN</code> edges to
<code>n:Hypothesis</code> nodes (candidate answers) and <code>BLOCKED_BY</code> edges to
<code>n:Failure</code>/<code>n:Limitation</code> nodes. <strong>Gap detection</strong> (Section 4.7) is partly
the automated discovery of under-connected <code>n:Question</code> nodes.</p>
<hr />
<h2 id="3-verification-framework">3. VERIFICATION FRAMEWORK</h2>
<h3 id="31-the-nine-epistemic-tiers">3.1 The nine epistemic tiers</h3>
<p>Every claim node carries exactly one <code>epistemic_tier</code>. Ordered strongest → weakest:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Tier</th>
<th>Name</th>
<th>Definition</th>
<th>Example</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>T1</td>
<td><strong>Verified Fact</strong></td>
<td>Independently reproduced, consensus, no credible dispute</td>
<td>Water boils at 100°C at 1 atm</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>T2</td>
<td><strong>Math Proof</strong></td>
<td>Formal proof, machine-checkable (Lean/Coq) or accepted</td>
<td>√2 is irrational</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>T3</td>
<td><strong>Theory</strong></td>
<td>Well-supported explanatory framework, not directly observable</td>
<td>Evolution by natural selection</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>T4</td>
<td><strong>Observation</strong></td>
<td>Direct measurement/observation, possibly unrepeated</td>
<td>A supernova sighting</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>T5</td>
<td><strong>Expert Opinion</strong></td>
<td>Credible authority's judgment, no formal proof</td>
<td>A surgeon's protocol recommendation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>T6</td>
<td><strong>Personal Experience</strong></td>
<td>First-hand account, unverifiable by others</td>
<td>"This bridge design failed in my project"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>T7</td>
<td><strong>Hypothesis</strong></td>
<td>Testable conjecture, awaiting evidence</td>
<td>A proposed dark-matter candidate</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>T8</td>
<td><strong>Unverified</strong></td>
<td>Claim with weak/absent provenance</td>
<td>Forum post, hearsay</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>T9</td>
<td><strong>Speculation</strong></td>
<td>Opinion/philosophy without empirical anchor</td>
<td>"AI will solve climate change"</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Rules:</strong>
- Tiers are <em>per-claim</em>, not per-document. A paper can contain T2 math and
  T8 speculation; each proposition gets its own node.
- Tiers can be <strong>promoted</strong> (T7 → T3 after evidence accumulates) or
  <strong>demoted</strong> (T3 → T7 if contradicted). Promotion/demotion is logged.
- T6 (personal experience) is <strong>never deleted</strong> but is visually and
  algorithmically separated from T1–T5 in retrieval — a search can request
  "only verified" or "include experiences."</p>
<h3 id="32-source-verification-pipeline">3.2 Source verification pipeline</h3>
<p>Every ingested source passes through:</p>
<pre><code>INGEST → AUTHORSHIP CHECK → PROVENANCE CHAIN → EVIDENCE EVAL →
TIER ASSIGNMENT → CROSS-CHECK → CANONICAL INDEX
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li><strong>Authorship check:</strong> is the claimed author real? (ORCID, institutional
  affiliation, publication record). Detects impersonation and sockpuppetry.</li>
<li><strong>Provenance chain:</strong> document → raw data → experimental apparatus →
  raw observations → processed results → published claim. Broken chain =
  tier capped at T8.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence evaluation:</strong> for each <code>n:Evidence</code> node, record
  <code>replication_count</code>, <code>independent_replications</code>, <code>retraction_status</code>,
  <code>funding_conflicts</code>, <code>sample_size</code>, <code>methodology_rigor_score</code>.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="33-contradiction-detection">3.3 Contradiction detection</h3>
<p>Contradictions are <strong>features, not bugs</strong>. The archive stores them:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>CONTRADICTS</code> edges link incompatible claims.</li>
<li><strong>Contradiction clusters</strong> are <code>n:Controversy</code> nodes that aggregate
  mutually-contradicting claims (e.g., "climate sensitivity range").</li>
<li>Each controversy node has a <code>resolution_status</code>: <code>open</code>, <code>resolved_for_X</code>,
  <code>unresolved</code>, <code>resolved_by_consensus</code>, <code>resolved_by_experiment</code>.</li>
<li>Contradictions are surfaced in retrieval: a query about a contested topic
  returns <em>both sides</em> plus the controversy node, never silently picks one.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="34-outdated-knowledge-correction">3.4 Outdated-knowledge correction</h3>
<p>Knowledge decays. The archive handles it via:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Temporal validity:</strong> <code>valid_from</code>/<code>valid_until</code> on claims.</li>
<li><strong>Supersession chains:</strong> <code>SUPERSEDES</code> edges; the old node is never deleted
  but is marked <code>superseded_by</code> and demoted.</li>
<li><strong>Deprecation events:</strong> when a field overturns (e.g., a drug recalled, a
  cosmology model revised), a <code>DeprecationEvent</code> cascades: demote all
  dependent claims by one tier, flag for re-review.</li>
<li><strong>Re-verification schedule:</strong> high-impact claims (medical, safety,
  structural) get periodic re-verification triggers. Stale claims decay in
  ranking.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="35-uncertainty-representation">3.5 Uncertainty representation</h3>
<p>Every node carries a structured <code>uncertainty</code> object:</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">uncertainty:
  epistemic_tier: T4
  confidence: 0.72            # 0..1 aggregate
  known_unknowns:             # what we don't know
    - &quot;exact replication count beyond 3&quot;
  contested_by: [n:Controversy#climate-sensitivity]
  last_verified: 2024-06-01
  decay_half_life_days: 1825  # how fast this claim ages
</code></pre>
<p>The AI engine (Section 4) <strong>must surface uncertainty</strong> in every answer —
an answer without an uncertainty statement is a bug, not a feature.</p>
<hr />
<h2 id="4-ai-knowledge-engine">4. AI KNOWLEDGE ENGINE</h2>
<h3 id="41-capabilities-the-ten">4.1 Capabilities (the ten)</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>#</th>
<th>Capability</th>
<th>Mechanism</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>1</td>
<td><strong>Semantic search</strong></td>
<td>Dense vector retrieval over chunked nodes + BM25 hybrid</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td><strong>Graph reasoning</strong></td>
<td>Cypher/GQL traversals over the knowledge graph</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td><strong>RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)</strong></td>
<td>Graph-grounded retrieval → LLM synthesis with citations</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>4</td>
<td><strong>NLU (Natural Language Understanding)</strong></td>
<td>Entity/relation extraction from text into graph nodes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>5</td>
<td><strong>Cross-disciplinary discovery</strong></td>
<td>Graph paths connecting distant domains (Section 4.5)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>6</td>
<td><strong>Pattern recognition</strong></td>
<td>Graph motif detection, embedding clustering</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>7</td>
<td><strong>Hypothesis generation</strong></td>
<td>LLM + graph completion: propose edges/nodes filling gaps</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>8</td>
<td><strong>Gap detection</strong></td>
<td>Under-connected questions, missing evidence, contradictions</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>9</td>
<td><strong>Outcome learning</strong></td>
<td>Track which retrieved answers were useful/correct → rerank</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>10</td>
<td><strong>Provenance tracing</strong></td>
<td>Every AI answer cites node IDs → full evidence chain</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3 id="42-retrieval-architecture-hybrid-four-channels">4.2 Retrieval architecture (hybrid, four channels)</h3>
<pre><code>Query
 ├─→ [C1] Dense vector search      (semantic similarity, pgvector/Qdrant/LanceDB)
 ├─→ [C2] Sparse BM25               (keyword, Postgres FTS / Elasticsearch)
 ├─→ [C3] Graph traversal           (Cypher: expand from seed entities)
 └─→ [C4] Structured filter         (epistemic_tier, domain, date, language)
        │
        ▼
   Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)
        │
        ▼
   Cross-encoder reranker (optional, for high-stakes queries)
        │
        ▼
   Context assembly → LLM synthesis → answer + citations + uncertainty
</code></pre>
<h3 id="43-graph-rag-pattern-the-key-innovation">4.3 Graph-RAG pattern (the key innovation)</h3>
<p>Standard RAG retrieves text chunks. <strong>Graph-RAG</strong> retrieves <em>structured
knowledge neighborhoods</em>:</p>
<ol>
<li>Extract entities from the query ("tokamak", "fusion gain").</li>
<li>Find seed nodes in the graph.</li>
<li>Traverse <code>k</code> hops collecting: the node, its <code>n:Evidence</code>, its
   <code>n:Experiment</code>s, its <code>n:Failure</code>s, its <code>n:Lesson</code>s, its
   <code>SUPERSEDES</code>/<code>CONTRADICTS</code> neighbors.</li>
<li>Serialize the subgraph into a prompt with node IDs as citation anchors.</li>
<li>LLM answers grounded in the subgraph, citing node IDs.</li>
</ol>
<p>This is the difference between "what does the internet say about tokamaks"
and "here are the 7 documented tokamak failure modes, each with its
experiment ID and lesson node."</p>
<h3 id="44-anti-hallucination-contract-mandatory">4.4 Anti-hallucination contract (mandatory)</h3>
<p><strong>Every AI-generated answer must satisfy:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Citation requirement:</strong> every factual claim in the answer maps to ≥1
   node ID that exists in the graph and is retrievable. Uncited claims are
   stripped or marked <code>[UNSOURCED]</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence floor:</strong> answers to empirical questions must cite <code>n:Evidence</code>
   nodes; pure-LLM generation is forbidden for empirical claims.</li>
<li><strong>Uncertainty disclosure:</strong> every answer ends with the aggregate
   uncertainty of its cited nodes (lowest tier wins).</li>
<li><strong>Tier respect:</strong> the engine never presents T6/T8/T9 material as T1–T3.
   Experience reports are labeled "personal account — not independently verified."</li>
<li><strong>Refusal:</strong> if retrieval returns no supporting nodes, the engine says
   "the archive does not contain verified knowledge on this" — it does not
   confabulate.</li>
<li><strong>Adversarial check:</strong> generated answers are re-verified against the
   source nodes before display (a second pass checking that citations
   actually support the claims).</li>
</ol>
<h3 id="45-cross-disciplinary-discovery">4.5 Cross-disciplinary discovery</h3>
<p>The graph enables <strong>serendipitous retrieval</strong>: paths between distant domains.</p>
<p>Example mechanism — <strong>analogical bridge search</strong>:
- Query: "how to improve battery thermal management"
- Graph finds <code>n:Concept:Phase-Change Material</code> in <code>Domain: Materials Science</code>
- Finds <code>n:Concept:Regenerative Cooling</code> in <code>Domain: Aerospace Engineering</code>
- Finds shared <code>n:Principle:Latent Heat Absorption</code>
- Surfaces the aerospace method as a candidate for batteries →
  generates a <code>n:Hypothesis</code> node for human review.</p>
<p>Implementation: weighted shortest-path / random-walk-with-restart over the
graph, seeded from query entities, filtered by <code>epistemic_tier ≤ T5</code>,
scored by path novelty (penalize paths within same discipline).</p>
<h3 id="46-failure-first-retrieval-the-killer-feature">4.6 Failure-first retrieval (the killer feature)</h3>
<p>For any <code>n:Method</code> or <code>n:Experiment</code> query, retrieval <strong>always includes</strong>:
- All <code>n:Failure</code> nodes connected via <code>INVALIDATES</code> or sharing the method.
- All <code>n:Lesson</code> nodes distilled from those failures.
- A "known failure modes" summary block at the top of the answer.</p>
<p>A user designing a bridge gets the collapse records <em>first</em>. This is the
archive's reason to exist.</p>
<h3 id="47-gap-detection">4.7 Gap detection</h3>
<p>Automated processes that find holes in human knowledge:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Under-connected questions:</strong> <code>n:Question</code> nodes with no <code>n:Hypothesis</code>
  children, or hypotheses with no <code>n:Experiment</code> children.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence deserts:</strong> <code>n:Principle</code> nodes with claims but zero <code>n:Evidence</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Contradiction clusters</strong> with no active <code>n:Experiment</code> attempting
  resolution.</li>
<li><strong>Temporal gaps:</strong> fields with no new evidence in &gt;N years.</li>
<li><strong>Cross-domain gaps:</strong> principles proven in domain A with no attempted
  application in analogous domain B.</li>
</ul>
<p>Gap reports are published as <code>n:Report</code> nodes — a continuously updated
<strong>map of what humanity doesn't know</strong>, which is itself valuable knowledge.</p>
<h3 id="48-outcome-learning">4.8 Outcome learning</h3>
<p>Every AI answer interaction is logged (privacy-preserving, Section 7):
- Was the answer useful? (thumbs, follow-up query, citation click-through)
- Was a cited node later found wrong? (feeds back to demotion)
- Did the user act on a <code>n:Lesson</code> and avoid a failure? (the holy grail —
  tracked via optional "I used this" reports)</p>
<p>This closes the loop: the archive learns which knowledge <em>prevents harm</em>.</p>
<hr />
<h2 id="5-experience-record-schema">5. EXPERIENCE RECORD SCHEMA</h2>
<h3 id="51-the-canonical-record">5.1 The canonical record</h3>
<p>Every significant human experience — a failed experiment, a field deployment,
a medical case, a historical event witnessed, a design iteration — is stored
as an <strong>Experience Record</strong> with this mandatory structure:</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">experience_record:
  id: exp:uuid
  title: &quot;J-TEXT-0042: Li-S battery thermal runaway in pouch cells&quot;
  epistemic_tier: T6              # personal experience — never auto-promoted
  class: experiment_failure      # one of the 7 classes + experience subtypes

  # --- the spine ---
  problem: &quot;Pouch-cell Li-S batteries overheated during fast-charge cycling&quot;
  context:
    when: &quot;2023-2024&quot;
    where: &quot;lab, Shanghai&quot;
    conditions: &quot;25°C ambient, 2C charge rate, 80% SOC window&quot;
    participants: [person:uuid, org:uuid]
    prior_state: &quot;cells rated for 500 cycles&quot;
  objective: &quot;Achieve 1000 cycles at 2C without capacity fade &gt;20%&quot;

  # --- the attempt ---
  assumptions:
    - &quot;Electrolyte stable to 60°C&quot;
    - &quot;Separator prevents dendrite propagation&quot;
    - &quot;BMS cutoff at 55°C sufficient&quot;
  actions:
    - step: 1
      action: &quot;Charged 20 cells at 2C to 80% SOC&quot;
      tool: &quot;Neware BTS4000 cycler&quot;
      resource_cost: &quot;480 cell-hours&quot;
    - step: 2
      action: &quot;Accelerated aging at 45°C&quot;
      tool: &quot;thermal chamber&quot;
      resource_cost: &quot;720 cell-hours&quot;
  resources:
    materials: [&quot;Li-S pouch cells ×20&quot;, &quot;LiTFSI electrolyte&quot;]
    equipment: [&quot;BTS4000&quot;, &quot;thermal chamber&quot;, &quot;EIS analyzer&quot;]
    funding: &quot;internal R&amp;D&quot;
    personnel_hours: 340

  # --- the outcome ---
  results:
    quantitative:
      - metric: &quot;cycle life&quot;
        expected: &quot;&gt;1000 cycles&quot;
        actual: &quot;mean 312 cycles, σ=47&quot;
      - metric: &quot;failure mode&quot;
        actual: &quot;thermal runaway at cycle ~300, venting + ignition&quot;
    qualitative: &quot;cells swelled after cycle 250; EIS showed SEI growth&quot;
  failures:
    - what_failed: &quot;SEI layer grew exponentially above 40°C&quot;
      when_detected: &quot;cycle 180 (post-mortem EIS)&quot;
      root_cause_hypothesis: &quot;electrolyte oxidation at high temp + high rate&quot;
      could_have_been_predicted_by: [&quot;accelerated aging test at higher temp earlier&quot;]
  solutions_attempted:
    - &quot;reduced charge rate to 1C → extended life to 480 cycles (still insufficient)&quot;
    - &quot;added ceramic separator → delayed but did not prevent runaway&quot;
  solutions_that_worked: []        # empty = unresolved
  evidence:
    - type: &quot;raw_data&quot;
      location: &quot;dataset:uuid (cyclers logs, EIS spectra)&quot;
      reproducible: true
    - type: &quot;images&quot;
      location: &quot;artifact:uuid (post-mortem photos)&quot;
    - type: &quot;third_party&quot;
      location: &quot;paper:uuid (confirms similar failure in literature)&quot;
  lessons:
    - &quot;Li-S pouch cells need active cooling above 1C charge rates&quot;
    - &quot;SEI growth is the leading indicator — monitor EIS from cycle 50&quot;
    - &quot;Accelerated aging at 45°C predicts 25°C failure ~3x faster&quot;
  knowledge_links:
    methods_used: [method:uuid]
    principles_violated: [principle:lawson-criterion-analogue]
    related_failures: [exp:uuid (similar Li-ion thermal events)]
    contradicts: []
    supports: [hypothesis:uuid]

  # --- provenance ---
  author: person:uuid
  organization: org:uuid
  recorded_at: 2024-11-25
  verification:
    independently_replicated: false
    replication_status: &quot;single-lab&quot;
    peer_reviewed: false
    raw_data_available: true
</code></pre>
<h3 id="52-experience-subtypes">5.2 Experience subtypes</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Subtype</th>
<th>Example</th>
<th>Tier cap</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>lab_experiment</code></td>
<td>Failed synthesis, unexpected result</td>
<td>T6</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>field_deployment</code></td>
<td>Bridge inspection finding, mine collapse</td>
<td>T6</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>clinical_case</code></td>
<td>Patient outcome, adverse event</td>
<td>T6 (anonymized)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>engineering_incident</code></td>
<td>Factory failure, software outage postmortem</td>
<td>T6</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>historical_witness</code></td>
<td>Oral history, eyewitness account</td>
<td>T6</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>indigenous_knowledge</code></td>
<td>Traditional ecological knowledge, craft technique</td>
<td>T6 (with consent framework)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>negative_result</code></td>
<td>"We tried X, it didn't work, here's the data"</td>
<td>T6→can promote to T4 if replicated</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3 id="53-failed-experiments-as-first-class-citizens">5.3 Failed experiments as first-class citizens</h3>
<p>The schema <strong>requires</strong> the <code>failures:</code> block for any <code>class: experiment*</code>
record. A failure record with empty <code>failures</code> is rejected at ingestion —
this is the archive's core invariant.</p>
<p>Failed experiments get <strong>special retrieval treatment</strong>: they are indexed
under their <code>root_cause</code> and <code>lessons</code>, not just their <code>objective</code>, so a
search for "thermal runaway causes" finds them even if the user never
searched for "battery cycling."</p>
<h3 id="54-promotion-path-for-experiences">5.4 Promotion path for experiences</h3>
<p>A personal experience can gain epistemic weight:</p>
<pre><code>T6 (personal) → T4 (observation) → T3 (theory) → T1 (verified fact)
     │                │                │              │
     │                │                │              └─ consensus + replication
     │                │                └─ pattern across many experiences
     │                └─ independently replicated experiment
     └─ initial recording (always T6 or lower)
</code></pre>
<p>Promotion requires: independent replication, peer review, or convergence
with existing verified knowledge. <strong>Never automatic.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2 id="6-technical-architecture">6. TECHNICAL ARCHITECTURE</h2>
<h3 id="61-the-stack-layered">6.1 The stack (layered)</h3>
<pre><code>┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ L6 · PRESENTATION   Web app, mobile, API, offline PWA        │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ L5 · AI ENGINE      RAG orchestrator, graph reasoner, NLU    │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ L4 · KNOWLEDGE      Graph DB + Vector DB + Search + Relational│
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ L3 · PIPELINES      Ingestion, verification, translation,     │
│                     extraction, embedding, contradiction scan │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ L2 · STORAGE        Distributed object store + graph shards   │
│                     + vector shards + cryptographic ledger    │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ L1 · IDENTITY       DID/VC, signatures, reputation, access    │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</code></pre>
<h3 id="62-technology-selection-existing-vs-near-term-vs-speculative">6.2 Technology selection — existing vs near-term vs speculative</h3>
<p><strong>Legend:</strong> <code>[EXISTS]</code> production-ready today · <code>[NEAR]</code> stable but maturing ·
<code>[SPEC]</code> research / not yet practical.</p>
<h4 id="storage-databases">Storage &amp; databases</h4>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Component</th>
<th>Choice</th>
<th>Status</th>
<th>Rationale</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Relational (metadata, users, transactions)</td>
<td><strong>PostgreSQL 16+</strong> + <code>pgvector</code></td>
<td><code>[EXISTS]</code></td>
<td>One DB for relational + vector; ACID; mature</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Property graph (knowledge graph)</td>
<td><strong>Neo4j Community/Enterprise</strong> OR <strong>FalkorDB</strong></td>
<td><code>[EXISTS]</code></td>
<td>Neo4j: mature, Cypher, huge ecosystem. FalkorDB: Redis-based, fast traversals, GraphBLAS — better for read-heavy graph analytics. <strong>Avoid Kuzu</strong> — archived Oct 2025.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Vector search (dedicated, scale-out)</td>
<td><strong>Qdrant</strong> (server) or <strong>LanceDB</strong> (embedded)</td>
<td><code>[EXISTS]</code></td>
<td>Qdrant for production service; LanceDB for edge/offline/prototype</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Full-text / BM25</td>
<td><strong>PostgreSQL FTS</strong> or <strong>Elasticsearch/OpenSearch</strong></td>
<td><code>[EXISTS]</code></td>
<td>Postgres FTS suffices for prototype; OpenSearch at scale</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Object storage (documents, datasets, media)</td>
<td><strong>S3-compatible</strong> (MinIO self-hosted or cloud)</td>
<td><code>[EXISTS]</code></td>
<td>Content-addressed blobs, versioning</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Distributed file system (global scale)</td>
<td><strong>IPFS</strong> or <strong>Filecoin</strong></td>
<td><code>[EXISTS]</code>/<code>[NEAR]</code></td>
<td>Content-addressing = built-in integrity; IPFS for public knowledge, Filecoin for persistence incentives</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Immutable audit ledger</td>
<td><strong>Hash-chained append-only log</strong> (custom) or <strong>Apache Kafka + cryptographic chaining</strong></td>
<td><code>[EXISTS]</code></td>
<td>Every write to the archive is a signed, chained record — tamper-evident</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Time-series (access logs, metrics)</td>
<td><strong>TimescaleDB</strong> or <strong>VictoriaMetrics</strong></td>
<td><code>[EXISTS]</code></td>
<td>Usage analytics, outcome learning</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h4 id="ai-ml">AI / ML</h4>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Component</th>
<th>Choice</th>
<th>Status</th>
<th>Rationale</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Embeddings</td>
<td><strong>Open-source models</strong>: BGE-M3, E5, GTE (multilingual)</td>
<td><code>[EXISTS]</code></td>
<td>Multilingual, self-hostable, no API dependency</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>LLM (synthesis, extraction)</td>
<td><strong>Llama 3.x / Qwen 2.5 / Mistral</strong> (self-hosted) + optional API (Claude/GPT)</td>
<td><code>[EXISTS]</code></td>
<td>Self-hosted for sovereignty; API for quality ceiling</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>RAG orchestration</td>
<td><strong>LlamaIndex</strong> or <strong>LangGraph</strong> (custom)</td>
<td><code>[EXISTS]</code></td>
<td>LlamaIndex for retrieval pipelines; LangGraph for stateful agents</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>GraphRAG</td>
<td><strong>LightRAG</strong> or <strong>Microsoft GraphRAG</strong> (reference) + custom graph layer</td>
<td><code>[EXISTS]</code>/<code>[NEAR]</code></td>
